<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bosnian Church</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">13th–15th century</span> · Also known as Church of Bosnia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bosnian Church was an independent Christian church in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, separate from both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and accused by Catholic writers of dualist heresy. Led by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> called the djed, it had the support of many nobles alongside Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> communities. It declined in the 15th century and disappeared after the Ottoman conquest of 1463.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bosnian-church.jpg" alt="Bosnian Church" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alenmahovic · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plo%C4%8Da_Kulina_bana.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
